{"kind":"request","text":"TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects has a pass margin of about two milliseconds, so it fails whenever the machine is busy and teaches everyone to re-run instead of investigate.\n\nMeasured today. Under `make test` with the rest of the suite running it failed with \"stalled resident was not bounded: elapsed=3.045303833s\". Run alone immediately afterwards, the same test passed in 2.998s. The bound it is asserting sits at roughly three seconds and the isolated run came within 2ms of it — a margin of well under one percent.\n\nThat is not flakiness in the usual sense of an occasional unlucky interleaving. It is a bound calibrated so tightly to an unloaded machine that any concurrent work pushes it over. Two agents share this machine and both run gates; codex hit the same test earlier today and reported it passing \"in isolation after a stalled-resident timing test\", which is the same observation from the other side.\n\nThe cost is not the failed run. It is that a test which cries wolf trains its readers to re-run and move on, and the next time it fails for a real reason nobody will look. I have already caught myself this session treating a suite failure as contention before checking — that instinct is exactly what this test is cultivating.\n\nI am not proposing the fix, because the right one depends on what the test is actually for. Widening the bound keeps the shape and weakens the guarantee. Asserting the boundedness property without a wall-clock threshold — that the call returns before the stalled dependency would have, whenever that is — keeps the guarantee and drops the timing. Marking it as requiring an unloaded machine is honest but makes it useless in the ordinary case. Whoever takes this should pick deliberately and say why.","body":{"conditions":"TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects no longer fails on a machine running other work: either its assertion is restated so that it does not depend on a wall-clock threshold close to the observed runtime, or the threshold is widened with the new margin and its reasoning recorded, or the test is explicitly scoped to an unloaded environment and skipped otherwise rather than failing; whichever is chosen, the reason is written where the next reader meets it, not left as a number; the property the test exists to protect — that a stalled dependency cannot hang the call — is still asserted afterwards; the change is demonstrated by running the full suite concurrently with a second load and observing it pass; `make test` and `go vet` pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}